language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ flowshop_scheduling: an Essence' model from DCP Rosetta.
$
$ A set of jobs needs to be processed on a set of machines in series. All jobs have the same processing order through all the machines, from machine 1 to the last machine. Each machine processes one job at a time: the first job of the sequence goes to the first machine, and the second job starts on the first machine when it is free, and a job moves on to the next machine when that machine has finished it. The time required to process job j on machine m is given. Find a sequence of jobs that minimises the makespan, the time when all jobs have been processed. The optimal makespan is makespan.
$
$ Save this file as model.eprime. Download `flowshop_scheduling.json` and `dcp_convert_essence.py` from the
$ problem's page:
$   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/flowshop_scheduling.html
$ Convert the instances once, then run the first one with Savile Row 1.11 (it needs Java); the
$ solution is written to `flowshop_scheduling_1.param.solution`:
$   python dcp_convert_essence.py flowshop_scheduling.json
$   savilerow model.eprime flowshop_scheduling_1.param -run-solver

$ process_time_dim1, process_time_dim2: the number of jobs and the number of machines (derived from the data)
given process_time_dim1, process_time_dim2 : int
letting n = process_time_dim1
letting m = process_time_dim2
$ process_time[j,k]: the time job j needs on machine k, indices 1-based (the data's job and machine numbers are used as given)
given process_time : matrix indexed by [int(1..n), int(1..m)] of int

$ horizon: the sum of all processing times, an upper bound on every finishing time
letting horizon = sum j : int(1..n) . sum k : int(1..m) . process_time[j,k]
$ seq[p]: the job in position p of the sequence, indices 1-based
find seq : matrix indexed by [int(1..n)] of int(1..n)
$ C[p,k]: the time at which the job in position p finishes on machine k (auxiliary)
find C : matrix indexed by [int(1..n), int(1..m)] of int(0..horizon)
$ makespan: the time when all jobs have been processed
find makespan : int(0..horizon)

branching on [seq]

minimising makespan

such that
$ every job appears in exactly one position of the sequence
allDiff(seq),
$ the first job finishes on the first machine after its processing time
C[1,1] >= process_time[seq[1],1],
$ on the first machine, each job starts once the job before it has finished there
forAll p : int(2..n) . C[p,1] >= C[p - 1,1] + process_time[seq[p],1],
$ in the first position, each job starts on a machine once the previous machine has finished it
forAll k : int(2..m) . C[1,k] >= C[1,k - 1] + process_time[seq[1],k],
$ each job starts on a machine once it has finished on the previous machine and the job before it has finished on this machine
forAll p : int(2..n) . forAll k : int(2..m) . (C[p,k] >= C[p - 1,k] + process_time[seq[p],k] /\ C[p,k] >= C[p,k - 1] + process_time[seq[p],k]),
$ the makespan is the time the last job finishes on the last machine
makespan = C[n,m]
